For every bounded Jordan domain $D$ and every four distinct counterclockwise boundary marks $a,b,c,d$, one has $$\label{eq:cardy}
 \lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\mathbb P\bigl(\mathcal C_\varepsilon(D;a,b,c,d)\bigr)
 =F(x)
 :=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^x[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\,\mathrm du}
          {\displaystyle\int_0^1[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\,\mathrm du}
 =\frac{\Gamma(2/3)x^{1/3}}
        {\Gamma(1/3)\Gamma(4/3)}
        {}_2F_1\!\left(\frac13,\frac23;\frac43;x\right).$$

Let $D$ be a fixed bounded nondegenerate open rectangle and let $a,b,c,d$ be its corners in counterclockwise order, with $ab$ and $cd$ the chosen opposite sides. Write $x_D$ for its conformal parameter above and set $$Q_\varepsilon(\eta)=\mathbb P^{\eta}\bigl(\mathcal C_\varepsilon(D;a,b,c,d)\bigr),
 \qquad \eta=X_\varepsilon.$$ If $\mathbb P_{\mathrm{geom},\varepsilon}$ and $\mathbb E_{\mathrm{geom},\varepsilon}$ denote probability and expectation for the Poisson geometry at mesh $\varepsilon$, then $$\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
 \mathbb E_{\mathrm{geom},\varepsilon}\bigl[|Q_\varepsilon-F(x_D)|^2\bigr]=0.$$ In particular, for every $t>0$, $\mathbb P_{\mathrm{geom},\varepsilon}(|Q_\varepsilon-F(x_D)|>t)\to0$. These limits use the geometry law at each mesh, without a coupling across meshes.

For distinct triangles $t,u$, $$\label{fields:orthogonal}
 \langle Z_t,Z_u\rangle=0,
 \qquad \|Z_t\|^2=\frac34.$$

For every fixed bounded box $B\subset\mathbb C$ and every $a>0$, the full-plane model of spacing $\varepsilon$ satisfies $$\label{fields:closed}
 \lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
 \mathbb E\!\sum_{\substack{P\text{ a rim},\ P\subset B\\\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(P)\ge a}}
                   |Q_P(1)|^2=0.$$ The corresponding full sums for $V_P$ vanish identically.

The following facts hold for the full-plane model of spacing $\varepsilon$.*

1.  *On a fixed compact set, the Delaunay triangulation and the Voronoi cells meeting it are determined by the points in any fixed positive collar, outside an event of probability tending to zero. Their local diameters tend to zero in probability. The same determination statement holds when the outside point configuration is changed while the collar is retained.*

2.  *For a unit square at spacing one, the probability that an incident triangle has circumradius greater than $R>2$ is at most $Ce^{-cR^2}$. The same form of bound holds for a Voronoi cell meeting the square whose site has distance greater than $R$ from it. Counts of incident triangles and sums of any fixed polynomial diameter weights have moments of all orders.*



3.  *If $S$ is a square of side $s$, $k\ge0$, and $1\le p<\infty$, then $$\label{prob:mesh-moments}
     \left\|\sum_{t\cap S\ne\varnothing}\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(t)^k\right\|_{L^p}
       \le C_{p,k}\varepsilon^{k-2}(s+\varepsilon)^2.$$ For a deterministic Borel set $E$ of finite area, $$\label{prob:barycenter-intensity}
     \mathbb E\sum_{z_t\in E}\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(t)^k
        =m_k\varepsilon^{k-2}\mathop{\mathrm{Area}}(E),\qquad m_k<\infty.$$ At every fixed $s>0$, the sum of $\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(t)^2$ over triangles with barycenters in $S$ converges in every finite moment norm to $m_2s^2$ as $\varepsilon\downarrow0$. The sum over triangles meeting $S$ has the same limit. The corresponding statements hold for translation-covariant weights of degree two bounded by a constant times $\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}(t)^2$, such as $|p_t|^2$ and $|q_t|^2$.*

4.  *Within fixed positive margins, locally determined simple primal cycles can approximate a fixed circle arbitrarily closely and in its cyclic order. Finitely many separated circles admit disjoint such cycles.

For some $c>0$ and $C<\infty$, $$\label{prob:mixed-four}
 \limsup_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
 \mathbb P\left(\begin{array}{c}
 \text{four vertex-disjoint primal arms across }B(x,S)\setminus B(x,s),\\
 \text{with both colors represented}
 \end{array}\right)
 \le C(s/S)^{1+c}.$$ The four colors need not alternate.

For some $c>0$ and $C<\infty$, and the same fixed-scale convention, $$\label{prob:near-return}
 \limsup_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
 \mathbb P\left(\begin{array}{c}
 \text{some rim has two visits to }B(x,s)\text{ such that both}\\
 \text{complementary rim arcs between the visits leave }B(x,S)
 \end{array}\right)
 \le C(s/S)^{2+c}.$$ The rim can be part of a global spherical completion. The event is bounded by a local monochromatic-arm test, without a union over rims.

From every sequence $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ one can extract a subsequence and a complex number $a$, depending only on that subsequence, such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Re}}a<1/2$ and the following holds in every bulk patch. If $f\in C_c^\infty(B(x,R))$ and $\overline B(x,6R)$ lies in the patch, then $$\label{transport:bulk}
 \max_{P:\,P\not\subset B(x,4R)}
       |Q_P(f)-aV_P(f)|\xrightarrow[\varepsilon\to0]{\mathbb P}0.$$ As in the definition of a bulk patch, local tests may be set to zero on a geometry event whose probability tends to zero. The subsequence and scalar are common to all patches and tests.
